V. Kampen mod Humbaba
V. The fight with Humbaba

De stod og stirrede ind i skoven. De så op langs cedrenes stammer og spejdede ned ad vejen ind mellem træerne. Hvor Humbaba plejede at gå, løb en sti. Lige var hans spor, god at gå på var vejen. De så Cederbjerget, de udødeliges bolig, Irninis helligdom. Cederen rejste sig stolt mod bjerget. Under den lå en dejlig skygge, og buskene bredte sig […] cederen […] røgelsen […]. [Resten af første spalte og omtrent tyve linjer i den næste er stærkt beskadiget.]
They stood staring into the forest. They looked up the cedars and searched the avenue leading in among the trees. A path ran where Humbaba used to walk. His tracks were straight and the passage easy. They saw Cedar Mountain, home of the immortals, the sanctuary of Irnini. The cedar stood proud against the mountain. Its shade was lovely, full of delight, and the bushes spread beneath it […] the cedar […] incense […]. [The rest of the first column and about twenty lines in the next are badly damaged.]

»Så fik jeg endnu en drøm, min ven. Det andet syn var smukt. Vi to stod højt på en bjergtop. Så væltede bjerget, og vi blev som […] der fødes på steppen.« Enkidu tydede drømmen for sin ven: »Det syn bringer os held. Din drøm lover os stor gevinst. Bjerget, du så, må være Humbaba [?]. Vi fanger Humbaba og kaster hans krop til jorden. Hans lig bliver liggende i støvet. I morgen skal jeg vise dig, hvordan det går.«
“Then I had another dream, friend. This second vision was a pleasant one. We stood high on a mountain peak. Then the mountain toppled and left us like […] born on the steppe.” Enkidu interpreted his friend's dream. “That vision means good fortune for us. Your dream promises great gain. The mountain you saw must be Humbaba [?]. We'll capture Humbaba and throw his body down. His corpse will lie in the dust. Tomorrow I'll show you how it ends.”

Efter fyrre mil tog de en bid mad. Efter tres mil gjorde de holdt og gravede en fordybning i solen […]. Gilgamesh steg op over den og hældte mel ud til bjerget: »Bjerg, giv mig en drøm […] ånd på ham […]«. Bjerget gav en drøm […] åndede på ham […]. En kold vind rejste sig, et vindstød fór hen over […] fik ham til at krybe sammen […] han svajede som kornet på bjergsiden […]. Gilgamesh sank i knæ, satte sig på hælene, og søvnen kom over ham, som den kommer over mennesker. Ved midnat vågnede han brat og skyndte sig hen til sin ven.
After forty leagues they had a bite to eat. After sixty they rested and dug a hollow in the sun […]. Gilgamesh climbed above it and poured out flour for the mountain. “Mountain, grant me a dream […] breathe on him […].” The mountain gave a dream […] breathed on him […]. A cold wind rose and a gust swept across […] made him cower […] he swayed like grain on a mountainside […]. Gilgamesh bent his knees and sat back on his heels. Sleep came over him as it comes over people. At midnight he woke abruptly and hurried to his friend.

»Kaldte du på mig, min ven? Hvorfor er jeg så vågen? Rørte du ved mig? Hvorfor er jeg bange? Gik der en ånd forbi? Hvorfor ryster hele min krop? En tredje drøm har jeg haft, min ven, og denne drøm var frygtelig. Himlen brølede, jorden gav genlyd. Dagen formørkedes, mørket steg, lynene flammede. Ilden blussede op, pesten [?] svulmede, døden åd sig mæt. Så døde skæret hen, og flammerne svandt. De glødende brændestykker faldt til jorden som aske. Kom, lad os gå ned på steppen og tale sammen.« Enkidu begyndte at tyde drømmen for Gilgamesh […]. [Resten af spalten mangler.]
“Did you call me, friend? Why am I awake? Did you touch me? Why am I afraid? Did a spirit pass by? Why is my whole body shaking? I had a third dream, friend, and this one was dreadful. The sky roared, the earth rang with the sound. Day darkened, darkness rose, lightning flashed. Flames sprang up, pestilence [?] swelled and death ate its fill. Then the glare died away, the fires faded. The burning brands fell to earth as ashes. Come down to the steppe with me and let's talk it over.” Enkidu began interpreting the dream for Gilgamesh […]. [The rest of the column is lost.]

[På en anden tavle fra Hattusa fortæller Gilgamesh:] »Foruden min første drøm en anden […] I drømmen, min ven, et bjerg […] han kastede mig ned og greb om mine fødder […] Lyset blev stærkere. En mand […] smukkere end nogen anden i hele landet […] Han trak mig frem under bjerget […] gav mig vand at drikke, og min tørst blev slukket. Han satte mine fødder på jorden […].« Enkidu […] til denne gud […] sagde til Gilgamesh: »Min ven, vi går […] alt det fjendtlige […] ikke bjerget […] Kom, læg frygten af dig […].« [Efter omkring syv brudte linjer ophører denne version.]
[On another tablet from Hattusa Gilgamesh recounts:] “Besides my first dream, a second […] In my dream, friend, a mountain […] he threw me down and seized my feet […] The light grew brighter. A man […] more beautiful than anyone in the land […] He drew me out from under the mountain […] gave me water to drink, and my thirst was quenched. He set my feet on the ground […].” Enkidu […] to this god […] said to Gilgamesh, “My friend, we'll go […] all that is hostile […] not the mountain […] Come, put aside fear […].” [This version breaks off after about seven damaged lines.]

[Fjerde spalte mangler, og næsten intet af femte er bevaret. I den sjette er næsten hele kampen gået tabt. Den følgende passage kommer fra en hittitisk version.] På denne måde […] himlens solgud […] træerne. Han så Gilgamesh […] himlens solgud […] og viste ham diget ved grøfterne. Gilgamesh bad til himlens solgud: »Se, den dag til byen […] som er i byen. Jeg beder til himlens solgud. Jeg har begivet mig på vej […].«
[The fourth column is lost, and almost nothing of the fifth survives. In the sixth, nearly the whole fight is missing. The following passage comes from a Hittite version.] In this manner […] the sun god of heaven […] the trees. He saw Gilgamesh […] the sun god of heaven […] and showed him the dam by the ditches. Gilgamesh prayed to the sun god of heaven. “See, on that day to the city […] which is in the city. I pray to the sun god of heaven. I have set out on the road […].”

Himlens solgud hørte Gilgamesh. Han sendte mægtige vinde mod Humbaba: en stor vind, en nordenvind, en søndenvind, en uvejrsstorm og en stormvind, en kold vind, en hvirvelvind og en ond vind. Otte vinde rejste han. De greb Humbaba forfra og bagfra. Han kunne hverken komme frem eller tilbage. Så overgav Humbaba sig.
The sun god of heaven heard Gilgamesh. He sent mighty winds against Humbaba: a great wind, a north wind, a south wind, a tempest and a storm wind, a cold wind, a whirlwind and an evil wind. Eight winds he raised. They seized Humbaba front and back. He could go neither forward nor back. Then Humbaba surrendered.

Humbaba sagde til Gilgamesh: »Gilgamesh, hold inde! Vær min herre, så skal jeg tjene dig. Glem de pralende ord, jeg slyngede ud mod dig. Tungt […] jeg ville lægge mig […] og paladset.« Da sagde Enkidu til Gilgamesh: »Det tilbud, Humbaba kommer med, må du aldrig tage imod. Humbaba må ikke leve […]«. [Her bryder den hittitiske tavle af. Seks beskadigede linjer afslutter den assyriske version.] […] de huggede hovedet af Humbaba.
Humbaba said to Gilgamesh, “Gilgamesh, stay your hand! Be my master and I'll serve you. Forget the boasts I hurled at you. Weighty […] I would lie down […] and the palace.” Then Enkidu said to Gilgamesh, “You must never accept what Humbaba offers. Humbaba must not live […].” [The Hittite tablet breaks off here. Six damaged lines close the Assyrian version.] […] they cut off Humbaba's head.
